" = probably a drug abbreviation from the original study) vs DMSO. Top mechanism from the Reactome enrichment is probably DNA replication / cell cycle / DNA damage response. After looking at the results, I'll write the mechanism_call with a deterministic keyword-based classifier over significantly enriched terms, choosing the dominant family:\n\nFamilies (in order of priority, based on the count of significant terms and the smallest adjusted p-value):\n1. DNA replication / cell cycle / mitosis\n2. DNA repair / DNA damage response\n3. Apoptosis / programmed cell death / p53\n4. Immune / interferon / cytokine signaling\n5. Metabolism\n6. Extracellular matrix / adhesion / migration\n7. Transcription / translation / generic gene expression\n8. Other\n\nClassifier: keyword match against term names. primary_mechanism string is composed like: \"<family label> (driven by top Reactome terms: ...)\".\n\nFor honesty, include both a machine-derived \"primary_mechanism\" and a curated \"mechanism_summary\". I'll finalize the wording after seeing the top terms (the script writes the JSON including the classification; I'll check the output and, if needed, adjust the classifier mapping once and rerun — since it's cheap, that's allowed).\n\nSchema for resource_manifest.json: keep all fields of the input manifest + add a \"verification\" object:\n{\n  \"verified_at\": \"...\",\n  \"gmt_sha256_verified\": true,\n  \"background_sha256_verified\": true,\n  \"mapping_sha256_verified\": true,\n  \"term_count_verified\": true,\n  \"background_gene_count_verified\": true,\n  \"gmt_union_equals_background\": true,\n  \"software\": {\"python\": ..., \"gseapy\": \"1.1.4\", \"pydeseq2\": \"0.5.4\"},\n  \"policy\": \"No library downloads or substitutions were performed; enrichment used only local frozen files.\"\n}\n\nNext, DE filtering details: keep genes with total counts across all 6 samples ≥ 10. PyDESeq2 also filters internally (genes with too-low counts / extreme outliers). Document this.\